main.cc contains the different tests and chooses which task to run.
mc.h contains the declarations of the Monte Carlo functions.
mc.cc contains the implementation of plain Monte Carlo, the LCG generator,
the Halton sequence, quasi-Monte Carlo, and recursive stratified sampling.

The code is compiled by running

make clean
make

This compiles the code, runs all parts of the homework, saves the numerical
results in text files, and generates the plots.

The files produced are:

circle_errors.txt          results for the unit-circle plain Monte Carlo test
ellipsoid_results.txt      result for the ellipsoid volume calculation
quasi_errors.txt           comparison between plain MC and quasi-random MC
singular_results.txt       comparison of LCG, std::mt19937 and Halton on the singular integral
stratified_results.txt     result from recursive stratified sampling

circle_errors.svg          plot for the unit-circle error scaling
quasi_errors.svg           plot comparing plain MC and quasi-random MC error scaling


Separately each task is run like this in the Makefile:
Task A3 is run with

./main A_circle > circle_errors.txt
gnuplot plot_circle_errors.gpi

Task A4 is run with

./main A_ellipsoid

Task B quasi-random circle comparison is run with

./main B_quasi > quasi_errors.txt
gnuplot quasi_errors.gpi

The difficult singular integral is run with

./main B_singular > singular_results.txt

Task C is run with

./main C

For task A1 and A2 the monte carlo integration is 
implemented using the code written in the assignment. 
I used chatgpt to translate the code to c++. 

For task A3 I will calculate the area of a unit circle and
plot the estimated error and the actual error as functions
of the number of sampling points and check whether the 
actual error scales as 1/sqrt(N).
This is done in main.cc where I created this test
as a void run_task_A_circle() so I can run it from the main
function separately from the other tests. 

We define a function f that is equal to 1 when
x^2 + y^2 <= 1 and equal to 0 when x^2 + y^2 > 1.
Integrating this function from -1 to 1 in x and y
should give pi which is the area of the unit circle. 

The script tries different values of N and the results are
plotted with the script plot_circle_errors.gpi

Task A4 says to calculate the volume of a 
three-dimensional ellipsoid. The program compares
the Monte Carlo result with the exact value of the
volume. 

Task B says to implement a multidimensional MC integrator
that uses low-discrepancy (quasi-random) sequences. 

For the van der corput sequence I use the code from the
lecture notes just translated to c++

For the multidimensional Halton sequence we need a list
of n prime numbers which we generate using the 
code also from the lecture notes just translated to c++.. 

The halton sequence code is also from the lecture notes.

In run_task_B_quasi_circle() the plain MC is compared with
the quasi-random MC of the unit circle. The results 
are plotted using quasi_errors.gpi

For quasi-Monte Carlo the usual statistical error estimate 
from the variance is not valid, because the points are 
correlated and not independent. Therefore I estimate the 
error by calculating the integral twice, using two 
different Halton sequences, and taking the difference 
between the two results.

Next the assignment says to calculate a specific
difficult integral. 

The integral in the assignment is written with variables 
going from 0 to pi, with dx/pi, dy/pi and dz/pi. In the 
code I instead integrate over [0,1]^3. This is the same 
integral after the change of variables

u = x/pi, v = y/pi, w = z/pi.

Then dx/pi becomes du, dy/pi becomes dv, and dz/pi becomes 
dw. This is why the code uses cos(pi*x[0]), cos(pi*x[1]) 
and cos(pi*x[2]) inside the function, even though the 
integration limits in the code are 0 to 1.

The following three methods are tested to calculate the
integral:
1. LCG - pseudo-random generator
2. std::mt19937
3. Halton quasi-random sequence
the results are in singular_results.txt
All three methods are close to the exact value but
not perfect. 

For task C we are asked to implement recursive 
stratified sampling. 
The method first samples a number of points in the full integration region.
Then it checks which coordinate direction gives the largest sub-variance.
The region is split in that direction, and the remaining sample points are
distributed between the two sub-regions. The algorithm is then called
recursively on the two smaller regions.

In main.cc this is tested on the unit circle integral and compared with
plain Monte Carlo.